SOAPBOX: Operated from an open field in windy weather, with a single 9.6 m
SOAPBOX: wooden mast, a 100 Ah LiFePO4 battery, and a thermos of hot tea.
SOAPBOX: The mast was built from four 2400 mm lengths of 34 x 34 mm timber.
SOAPBOX: Initially, the antenna was a three-element inverted-V wire Yagi
SOAPBOX: hybrid. The reflector and director hung at lower heights on ropes
SOAPBOX: running from the top of the only mast.
SOAPBOX: At times the parasitic elements were folded away, leaving a
SOAPBOX: simple, no longer resonant inverted-V with broader coverage.
SOAPBOX: Each antenna rotation cost approximately two hours of operating
SOAPBOX: time.
SOAPBOX: On the second evening, while turning the antenna toward the USA,
SOAPBOX: the reflector tangled around a mid-level mast guy rope, where it
SOAPBOX: remains stuck. From then on I could use only the simple inverted-V
SOAPBOX: or the inverted-V with a director, making USA contacts much more
SOAPBOX: difficult.
SOAPBOX: Rig: Yaesu FT-710 at 100 W. Feedline: 25 m RG213-UBX plus 9.8 m
SOAPBOX: Airborne-5, with a Diamond BU-50A balun.
SOAPBOX: Each morning I set up the operating equipment each evening I
SOAPBOX: packed it up and transported it away. The wooden shelter frame and
SOAPBOX: mast remained standing at the site throughout the contest.
